<reference_puzzle index="1" source="eval" puzzle_id="6e4f6532" lines="26-41">
<description>
Dock train sprites onto station groups by matching coupler count, then orient via divider-color markers.

Input structure:
- bg; colored stripes (edge + internal) partition grid into rectangular regions.
- Color 8 forms train bodies; color 9 marks both isolated stations and train-attached couplers.
- Stripe-colored pixels on trains are orientation markers.

Rule:
- Stations = 8-connected 9-clusters not adjacent to any 8. Trains = components containing 8.
- Match train to station group where |couplers| == |station size|.
- Rotate/flip train so marker pixels face the same-color boundary stripe of the target region.
- Translate so couplers align exactly onto station cells; erase original position.

Key insight: Coupler count selects the docking target; marker color encodes which boundary stripe to face, fixing the unique orientation.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="eval" puzzle_id="4c3d4a41" lines="15-28">
<description>
Fit a grey bar into a grey frame by aligning projections with colored columns, then stack colors above.

Input structure:
- bg, a grey structural color forming two components: a large rectangular frame (slot) and a smaller bar with upward projections.
- Colored pixels inside the slot mark target columns.

Rule:
- Move the bar upward into the slot so its bottom edge sits one cell above the slot's interior bottom.
- Shift it horizontally so the bar's upward projections align column-wise with the colored pixels in the slot.
- Stack the colored pixels immediately above the bar projections in each column.

Key insight: The bar's projections act as keys that must align with the colored-column "locks" in the slot.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="6a980be1" lines="46-61">
<description>
Project the framed periodic stripe pattern across the canvas and erase the unused frame cells.

Input structure
- A bg canvas with a one-cell-thick rectangular frame of one color.
- Inside the frame are two parallel full-interior marker bars of another color.
- A third color fills the region between the marker bars on periodically spaced rows or columns.

Rule
- Infer the periodic row or column mask from the third-color stripes inside the frame.
- Paint the frame color along those full rows or columns, extended to the grid boundary.
- Extend the marker bars across the full canvas and repaint the source-color stripe cells between them.

Key insight
- The outer frame is not preserved as a border; it supplies the color for the periodic stripes selected by the inner source pattern.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="996ec1f3" lines="16-31">
<description>
Compress the noisy four-region board split by a solid cross into a 3x3 color map.

Input structure:
- A single full row and a single full column have the same solid separator color.
- The separator cross divides the input into four nonempty rectangular regions.
- Each region has a unique dominant color, with other colors acting as noise.

Rule:
- Find the unique solid same-color row/column pair.
- Fill the middle row and middle column of the 3x3 output with the separator color.
- Put the unique dominant color of each quadrant into the corresponding corner.

Key insight:
- The input grid is a noisy enlarged version of the desired 3x3 layout; the cross marks the coarse cell boundaries.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="eval" puzzle_id="21897d95" lines="283-301">
<description>
Repaint regions from T-shaped control markers, erase the markers, and optionally rotate based on a color-9 L-marker.

Input structure:
- The grid is a mosaic of monochrome regions.
- Color 1 forms T-shaped control markers on region boundaries.
- Some controls use three singleton color-1 pixels around a colored center; that center is a literal replacement color.
- An optional 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker triggers 90-degree rotation of the output.

Rule:
- For a color-1 T marker, the single stem points at the destination region to repaint.
- The two diagonal cells behind the stem identify the source region; the destination gets that source region's original color.
- For a colored-center T marker, the same stem identifies the destination, but the center color is the replacement.
- All marker cells are filled with the final color of the source-side region.
- A 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker controls rotation: ┐ (corner top-right) → CCW 90°, ┌ (corner top-left) → CW 90°.

Key insight:
- The stem chooses what changes, while the two diagonals behind the stem choose where the marker itself belongs.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="8f215267" lines="7-21">
<description>
Count same-color patterns per frame and represent the tally as dots inside.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Hollow rectangular frames in various colors.
- Small monochrome pattern sprites scattered outside frames.

Rule:
- Remove all small patterns from the grid.
- For each frame of color C, count how many patterns share color C.
- Draw that many dots along the frame's center row, spaced by 2, starting from the right.

Key insight: Frames act as tally displays — scattered same-color patterns are "absorbed" and their count is shown as interior dots.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="9b5080bb" lines="161-172">
<description>
Recolor the small defect bars on each shared-color object with the opposite panel color.

Input structure
- The grid contains two large panel colors and one shared object color.
- Each shared-color object is a mostly solid rectangle inside exactly one panel.
Rule
- Find the near-solid rectangular core of each object.
- Missing core pixels and one-cell protrusions define two-pixel bars perpendicular to the nearest core edge.
- Recolor those bars with the other panel color; leave all other pixels unchanged.
Key insight
- The colors to add are not tied to numeric ids: each object's surrounding panel identifies the opposite panel color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="c4d067a0" lines="9-23">
<description>
Complete a grid of colored blocks to match a minimap legend in the top-left corner.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Top-left corner: a small sprite of single colored pixels forming a minimap pattern on a regular lattice.
- Main area: uniformly colored square blocks (NxN) arranged on a regular grid; some positions are empty (bg).

Rule:
- The legend pixels define a color pattern; each pixel's lattice position maps to a block position in the main grid via a fixed scale factor and offset.
- Existing colored blocks confirm the scale/offset alignment.
- For every legend pixel whose corresponding grid position is empty, place a new block of that color.

Key insight: The top-left pixel pattern is a scaled-down minimap of the block layout; the mapping is inferred from blocks already present.
</description>
